{%- comment -%}
  In <head> so anything here applies before the grid is parsed.

  The engine writes its ranking into the collection itself (sortOrder MANUAL),
  so Shopify already serves products in the right order and the storefront has
  nothing to rearrange. That is the architecture: decide the order on the
  server, let the page load it, and the browser only renders.

  The grid is therefore NOT hidden by default. A veil only earns its delay when
  something is actually going to move, and on a managed collection nothing does
  - every shopper gets the server's order, which is already on screen. Hiding
  the grid for 2s to cover a reorder that will not happen is a slower page for
  no benefit, and was itself the thing that made the reorder visible: the veil
  released on a timer, before the data it was waiting for had arrived.

  widget.js hides the grid itself, for as long as it needs, only in the case
  that remains: when this shopper's own behaviour (which the server cannot
  know, as it lives in their sessionStorage) changes which products belong on
  the page. That is a small minority of loads.
{%- endcomment -%}
<script src="https://badgehq-widget.badgehq.workers.dev/widget.js" async></script>

{% schema %}
{
  "name": "BadgeHQ Widget",
  "target": "head",
  "settings": []
}
{% endschema %}
